{"id":"superflat-pop","title":"Superflat Pop","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Superflat_Pop","rev":240607,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Superflat Pop"]}," is an aesthetic and graphic design style that was prevalent from the late 2000s to early 2010s, inspired by Murakami's established art movement called ",{"t":"a","c":["Superflat"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Superflat"},". It is characterized by the use of vibrant color palettes, ",{"t":"a","c":["kawaii"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Kawaii"}," motifs, and funky clothing. It is often used in animation, video games, advertisements, and fashion. It bears similarities to ",{"t":"a","c":["Y2K Futurism"],"id":"y2k-futurism"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["McBling"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/McBling"},", ",{"t":"a","c":["Frutiger Aero"],"id":"frutiger-aero"}," and ",{"t":"a","c":["Vectordelia"],"id":"vectordelia"},", but focuses more on ",{"t":"a","c":["kawaii"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Kawaii"}," elements."]}],"sections":[{"title":"History","id":"history","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["Superflat was coined in 2000 by Japanese artist Takashi Murakami to describe his established art movement based on the flat aspect of traditional Japanese art with anime and manga craze of post-war Japanese society. He helped other artists to put Japan on the art world map and bridge the gap between fine art and commercial aesthetics, basically Japan's answer to ",{"t":"a","c":["Neo-Pop"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Neo-Pop"}," art."]},{"t":"p","c":["The term \"Superflat Pop\" was coined by the CARI (Consumer Aesthetics Research Institute) to refer to the Superflat style being adopted for commercial purposes, showing a more corporate-friendly form of the original art movement. The pop suffix was added in reference in pop culture according to CARI."]}]},{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["The visuals of Superflat Pop are inspired by the poppy and flowery graphics seen in some artworks by Murakami. The disturbing topics found in the original Superflat art movement are pretty much always discarded, for wider commercial appeal. It aims to reflect shallowness and the current state of Japanese pop culture, similar to what ",{"t":"a","c":["Pop Art"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Pop_Art"}," had been established from the late-1950s to the mid-1960s, as well as Neo-Pop art."]},{"t":"p","c":["The key features of this aesthetic are:"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Geometric shapes"]},{"c":["Vibrant (either neon or pastel) color palettes"]},{"c":["Strong and bold outlines, usually colored black (although it can be other colors)"]},{"c":["3D visuals"]},{"c":["Kawaii motifs"]},{"c":["\"Sweet and Sunny\" flowers"]},{"c":["Graffiti"]},{"c":["UPA Revival influenced visuals"]},{"c":["Urban vinyl figures and sculptures"]},{"c":[{"t":"a","c":["Otaku"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Otaku"}," culture"]},{"c":["Fonts like Roundhouse font from House Industries, VAG Rounded, Billo by Fontalicious, along with other rounded fonts between the 90s to early 2010s"]}]}]},{"title":"Fashion","id":"fashion","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Neff"]},{"c":["Bape"]},{"c":[{"t":"a","c":["Louis Vuitton x Murakami"],"href":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JP-bp_3JHRk"}," (Superflat Monogram)"]},{"c":["Paul Frank"]},{"c":["Terratag"]},{"c":["UNIQLO UT"]},{"c":[{"t":"a","c":["COACH Japan x Poppy-Chan Collection"],"href":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F3vWbMYXg30"}]},{"c":["Bobby Jack"]}]}]},{"title":"Media","id":"media","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"i","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Note: Due to the amount of media in this aesthetic, it has been reduced for the sake of the page. Here's a few examples noted here."]}]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Television"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Atomic Betty (Cartoon) (2004-2008)"]},{"c":["Cardcaptor Sakura (Anime) (1998-2000)"]},{"c":["Doodlebops Rockin’ Road Show (C